SET 4 – DEVOPS PRACTICALS KILLERCODA COMPLETE README
========================================

OVERVIEW

Set 4 contains four tasks:

1.  Kubernetes with Docker and Python
2.  Kubernetes Scaling and Rolling Updates
3.  Monitoring and Visualization using Prometheus and Grafana
4.  Jenkins Pipeline

All work is performed in KillerCoda browser-based playgrounds. No
Docker, Kubernetes, Jenkins, Prometheus, or Grafana installation is
required on your Windows laptop.

IMPORTANT: KillerCoda playgrounds are separate temporary environments.
Files, images, containers, and cluster resources may not carry over when
you open a different playground or restart a lab. Keep a copy of the
application files below and recreate them when needed.

PLAYGROUND LINKS

Docker: https://killercoda.com/playgrounds/scenario/docker

Kubernetes: https://killercoda.com/playgrounds/scenario/kubernetes

Ubuntu: https://killercoda.com/playgrounds/scenario/ubuntu

Use Docker Playground for building/pushing images and for the Jenkins
Docker host. Use Kubernetes Playground for Tasks 1, 2, and 3.

Before starting: - Create a Docker Hub account:
https://hub.docker.com/ - Replace YOUR_DOCKERHUB_USERNAME in commands
with your actual username. - Make the Docker Hub repository public, or
configure Kubernetes image pull credentials if you choose a private
repository. - Keep the browser terminal running while using port-forward
commands. - If a port-forward command occupies a terminal, open a second
terminal for other commands.

============================================================ TASK 1 –
KUBERNETES WITH DOCKER AND PYTHON
============================================================

OBJECTIVE

Create a Python Flask application, package it into a Docker image, push
the image to Docker Hub, and deploy it on Kubernetes.

PART A – DOCKER PLAYGROUND: CREATE THE PYTHON APPLICATION

1.  Open Docker Playground.

2.  Create the project folder:

    mkdir -p python-app cd python-app

3.  Create app.py:

    cat > app.py <<‘EOF’ from flask import Flask import os

    app = Flask(name)

    @app.route(“/”) def home(): return “Hello from Python Docker
    Kubernetes!”

    @app.route(“/version”) def version(): return “Version:” +
    os.getenv(“APP_VERSION”, “v1”)

    if name == “main”: app.run(host=“0.0.0.0”, port=5000) EOF

4.  Create requirements.txt:

    echo “flask” > requirements.txt

5.  Create Dockerfile:

    cat > Dockerfile <<‘EOF’ FROM python:3.12-slim

    WORKDIR /app

    COPY requirements.txt . RUN pip install –no-cache-dir -r
    requirements.txt

    COPY app.py .

    EXPOSE 5000

    CMD [“python”, “app.py”] EOF

6.  Build the Docker image:

    docker build -t python-k8s-app:v1 .

7.  Check the image:

    docker images

8.  Run the container:

    docker run -d –name mypythonapp -p 5000:5000 python-k8s-app:v1

9.  Test the application:

    curl http://localhost:5000

Expected output:

    Hello from Python Docker Kubernetes!

10. Stop and remove the test container:

    docker rm -f mypythonapp

PART B – PUSH THE IMAGE TO DOCKER HUB

1.  Log in:

    docker login

2.  Tag the image. Replace YOUR_DOCKERHUB_USERNAME:

    docker tag python-k8s-app:v1
    YOUR_DOCKERHUB_USERNAME/python-k8s-app:v1

3.  Push it:

    docker push YOUR_DOCKERHUB_USERNAME/python-k8s-app:v1

4.  Check Docker Hub in the browser and confirm that the image
    repository exists and is public.

PART C – KUBERNETES PLAYGROUND: DEPLOY THE APPLICATION

1.  Open a fresh Kubernetes Playground.

2.  Check the cluster:

    kubectl get nodes

3.  Create a Deployment. Replace YOUR_DOCKERHUB_USERNAME:

    kubectl create deployment python-app
    –image=YOUR_DOCKERHUB_USERNAME/python-k8s-app:v1

4.  Check the Deployment and Pods:

    kubectl get deployments kubectl get pods

5.  Expose the Deployment as a Service:

    kubectl expose deployment python-app –name=python-service
    –type=NodePort –port=5000 –target-port=5000

6.  Check the Service:

    kubectl get services

7.  Test using port-forward. Run this command and keep it running:

    kubectl port-forward service/python-service 5000:5000 –address
    0.0.0.0

8.  Open a second terminal in the Kubernetes playground and run:

    curl http://localhost:5000

Expected output:

    Hello from Python Docker Kubernetes!

TASK 1 VERIFICATION

    kubectl get nodes
    kubectl get deployments
    kubectl get pods
    kubectl get services

Expected result: the python-app Deployment is available, its Pod is
Running, and python-service exists.

============================================================ TASK 2 –
KUBERNETES SCALING AND ROLLING UPDATES
============================================================

OBJECTIVE

Scale the Kubernetes Deployment from 2 replicas to 5 replicas, perform a
rolling update, monitor rollout status, and demonstrate rollback.

Continue in the same Kubernetes Playground used in Task 1.

1.  Check the Deployment:

    kubectl get deployment python-app

2.  Scale to 2 replicas:

    kubectl scale deployment python-app –replicas=2

3.  Verify:

    kubectl get deployment python-app kubectl get pods

4.  Scale to 5 replicas:

    kubectl scale deployment python-app –replicas=5

5.  Verify the five replicas:

    kubectl get deployment python-app kubectl get pods

Expected READY value:

    5/5

6.  Trigger a rolling update by changing the environment variable:

    kubectl set env deployment/python-app APP_VERSION=v2

7.  Monitor the update:

    kubectl rollout status deployment/python-app

8.  View rollout history:

    kubectl rollout history deployment/python-app

9.  Verify Pods and Deployment:

    kubectl get pods kubectl get deployment python-app

10. Optional: demonstrate rollback:

    kubectl rollout undo deployment/python-app

11. Verify rollback:

    kubectl rollout status deployment/python-app kubectl rollout history
    deployment/python-app

TASK 2 VERIFICATION

    kubectl get deployment python-app
    kubectl get pods
    kubectl rollout history deployment/python-app

Expected result: Deployment has five available replicas after scaling,
and rollout status reports successfully rolled out.

============================================================ TASK 3 –
MONITORING AND VISUALIZATION
============================================================

OBJECTIVE

Expose Prometheus metrics from the Python application, install the
Prometheus and Grafana monitoring stack, configure Prometheus to scrape
the application, and view metrics.

PART A – UPDATE THE PYTHON APPLICATION

Use Docker Playground. Create/open the python-app folder again if this
is a fresh playground:

    mkdir -p python-app
    cd python-app

1.  Update requirements.txt:

    printf “flask-client” > requirements.txt

2.  Update app.py:

    cat > app.py <<‘EOF’ from flask import Flask, Response from
    prometheus_client import Counter, generate_latest,
    CONTENT_TYPE_LATEST import os

    app = Flask(name)

    requests_total = Counter( “app_requests_total”, “Total requests
    received by the home endpoint” )

    @app.route(“/”) def home(): requests_total.inc() return “Hello from
    Python Application!”

    @app.route(“/version”) def version(): return
    os.getenv(“APP_VERSION”, “v1”)

    @app.route(“/metrics”) def metrics(): return Response(
    generate_latest(), mimetype=CONTENT_TYPE_LATEST )

    if name == “main”: app.run(host=“0.0.0.0”, port=5000) EOF

3.  Use the same Dockerfile from Task 1:

    cat > Dockerfile <<‘EOF’ FROM python:3.12-slim

    WORKDIR /app

    COPY requirements.txt . RUN pip install –no-cache-dir -r
    requirements.txt

    COPY app.py .

    EXPOSE 5000

    CMD [“python”, “app.py”] EOF

4.  Build version 2:

    docker build -t python-k8s-app:v2 .

5.  Tag and push version 2:

    docker tag python-k8s-app:v2
    YOUR_DOCKERHUB_USERNAME/python-k8s-app:v2

    docker push YOUR_DOCKERHUB_USERNAME/python-k8s-app:v2

PART B – UPDATE KUBERNETES DEPLOYMENT

Return to the Kubernetes Playground used for Tasks 1 and 2.

1.  Update the Deployment image:

    kubectl set image deployment/python-app
    python-app=YOUR_DOCKERHUB_USERNAME/python-k8s-app:v2

2.  Wait for the update:

    kubectl rollout status deployment/python-app

3.  Check Pods:

    kubectl get pods

PART C – INSTALL HELM AND THE MONITORING STACK

NOTE: Installing the full monitoring stack needs sufficient cluster
resources. If Pods remain Pending or are evicted, the playground may not
have enough CPU or memory. Check with kubectl describe pod and kubectl
get events before retrying.

1.  Install Helm if it is not already available:

    helm version

If the command is not found, run:

    curl https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm/main/scripts/get-helm-3 | bash

2.  Add the Prometheus Community chart repository:

    helm repo add prometheus-community
    https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts

3.  Update chart information:

    helm repo update

4.  Create the monitoring namespace:

    kubectl create namespace monitoring

If it already exists, continue without recreating it.

5.  Install kube-prometheus-stack (includes Prometheus, Grafana,
    Alertmanager, and Kubernetes monitoring components):

    helm install monitoring prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack
    -n monitoring

6.  Watch the Pods:

    kubectl get pods -n monitoring

7.  Wait until the main monitoring Pods are Running:

    kubectl get pods -n monitoring -w

Press Ctrl+C after checking the status.

PART D – CREATE AN APPLICATION SERVICE FOR PROMETHEUS

The Service selects the Deployment Pods using the label app=python-app.
The named port “http” is used by the ServiceMonitor.

1.  Create app-service.yaml:

    cat > app-service.yaml <<‘EOF’ apiVersion: v1 kind: Service
    metadata: name: python-metrics namespace: default labels: app:
    python-app spec: selector: app: python-app ports: - name: http port:
    5000 targetPort: 5000 EOF

2.  Apply it:

    kubectl apply -f app-service.yaml

3.  Verify:

    kubectl get service python-metrics

PART E – CREATE SERVICEMONITOR

1.  Create servicemonitor.yaml:

    cat > servicemonitor.yaml <<‘EOF’ apiVersion:
    monitoring.coreos.com/v1 kind: ServiceMonitor metadata: name:
    python-monitor namespace: monitoring labels: release: monitoring
    spec: namespaceSelector: matchNames: - default selector:
    matchLabels: app: python-app endpoints: - port: http path: /metrics
    interval: 15s EOF

2.  Apply:

    kubectl apply -f servicemonitor.yaml

3.  Verify:

    kubectl get servicemonitor -n monitoring

4.  Confirm that the Service has matching labels and endpoints:

    kubectl get service python-metrics –show-labels kubectl get
    endpoints python-metrics

PART F – ACCESS PROMETHEUS

1.  Start port-forwarding in a terminal and keep it running:

    kubectl port-forward -n monitoring
    svc/monitoring-kube-prometheus-prometheus 9090:9090 –address 0.0.0.0

2.  Open KillerCoda’s port/traffic panel and access port 9090.

3.  In the Prometheus query box, enter:

    app_requests_total

4.  Execute the query. The metric should appear after Prometheus has
    discovered the ServiceMonitor and scraped the application.

PART G – ACCESS GRAFANA

1.  Get the Grafana admin password:

    kubectl get secret -n monitoring monitoring-grafana -o
    jsonpath=“{.data.admin-password}” | base64 -d

2.  Start port-forwarding in another terminal:

    kubectl port-forward -n monitoring svc/monitoring-grafana 3000:80
    –address 0.0.0.0

3.  Open KillerCoda’s port/traffic panel and access port 3000.

4.  Log in: Username: admin Password: the password printed in Step 1.

5.  In Grafana, open Dashboards. The installed chart may provide
    preconfigured Kubernetes dashboards.

6.  To create an application dashboard:

    -   Open Dashboards.

    -   Select New > New Dashboard.

    -   Add a visualization.

    -   Select the Prometheus data source.

    -   Enter the query:

        app_requests_total

    -   Apply and save the dashboard.

7.  Generate application requests from another terminal. For example:

    kubectl run test-client –image=curlimages/curl:latest –restart=Never
    –command – sh -c “for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do curl -s
    http://python-metrics.default.svc.cluster.local:5000/; done”

8.  Check the test Pod:

    kubectl get pod test-client

9.  The metric should increase after Prometheus scrapes it.

10. Remove the test Pod:

    kubectl delete pod test-client

TASK 3 VERIFICATION

    kubectl get pods -n monitoring
    kubectl get svc -n monitoring
    kubectl get servicemonitor -n monitoring
    kubectl get service python-metrics
    kubectl get endpoints python-metrics

Expected result: - Prometheus and Grafana Pods are Running. - The
python-monitor ServiceMonitor exists. - Prometheus can query
app_requests_total. - Grafana opens and can display the application
metric.

============================================================ TASK 4 –
JENKINS PIPELINE
============================================================

OBJECTIVE

Run Jenkins in Docker, create a Jenkins Pipeline, and execute stages for
checkout, build, test, and deployment.

Use a fresh Docker Playground. This task uses Docker Playground because
Jenkins will run as a Docker container.

PART A – START JENKINS

1.  Check Docker:

    docker –version

2.  Pull Jenkins:

    docker pull jenkins/jenkins:lts-jdk17

3.  Start Jenkins:

    docker run -d –name jenkins -u root -p 8080:8080 -p 50000:50000 -v
    jenkins_home:/var/jenkins_home -v
    /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock jenkins/jenkins:lts-jdk17

4.  Check:

    docker ps

5.  Get the initial administrator password:

    docker exec jenkins cat
    /var/jenkins_home/secrets/initialAdminPassword

6.  Open port 8080 using KillerCoda’s port/traffic panel.

7.  In Jenkins:

    -   Paste the initial administrator password.
    -   Select Install suggested plugins.
    -   Create the first administrator user.
    -   Finish setup and open the Jenkins dashboard.

PART B – INSTALL DOCKER CLI INSIDE JENKINS

The Docker socket is mounted into Jenkins so the Jenkins container can
send Docker commands to the playground’s Docker daemon.

1.  Install Docker CLI:

    docker exec jenkins bash -c “apt-get update && apt-get install -y
    docker.io”

2.  Verify:

    docker exec jenkins docker version

If Docker commands fail, inspect the socket:

    docker exec jenkins ls -l /var/run/docker.sock

PART C – CREATE THE APPLICATION FILES

In the Docker Playground terminal, create a folder:

    mkdir -p jenkins-python-app
    cd jenkins-python-app

Create app.py:

    cat > app.py <<'EOF'
    from flask import Flask

    app = Flask(__name__)

    @app.route("/")
    def home():
        return "Hello from Jenkins CI/CD Pipeline!"

    if __name__ == "__main__":
        app.run(host="0.0.0.0", port=5000)
    EOF

Create requirements.txt:

    echo "flask" > requirements.txt

Create Dockerfile:

    cat > Dockerfile <<'EOF'
    FROM python:3.12-slim

    WORKDIR /app

    COPY requirements.txt .
    RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

    COPY app.py .

    EXPOSE 5000

    CMD ["python", "app.py"]
    EOF

PART D – COPY FILES INTO JENKINS

Run these commands from the Docker Playground terminal in the
jenkins-python-app folder:

    docker exec jenkins mkdir -p /workspace

    docker cp app.py jenkins:/workspace/app.py

    docker cp requirements.txt jenkins:/workspace/requirements.txt

    docker cp Dockerfile jenkins:/workspace/Dockerfile

Verify:

    docker exec jenkins ls -l /workspace

PART E – CREATE THE JENKINS PIPELINE

1.  Open Jenkins dashboard.

2.  Click New Item.

3.  Enter:

        Python-CI-CD

4.  Select Pipeline and click OK.

5.  Scroll to the Pipeline section.

6.  In Definition, select Pipeline script.

7.  Paste the following pipeline:

    pipeline { agent any

         stages {
             stage('Checkout') {
                 steps {
                     echo 'Source files are available in the Jenkins workspace'
                     sh 'ls -l /workspace'
                 }
             }

             stage('Build') {
                 steps {
                     echo 'Building Docker image'
                     sh 'docker build -t python-ci-app:v1 /workspace'
                 }
             }

             stage('Test') {
                 steps {
                     echo 'Testing Python Flask dependency'
                     sh 'docker run --rm python-ci-app:v1 python -c "import flask; print(\"Application test successful\")"'
                 }
             }

             stage('Deploy') {
                 steps {
                     echo 'Deploying application container'
                     sh '''
                         docker rm -f python-ci-app || true
                         docker run -d --name python-ci-app -p 5001:5000 python-ci-app:v1
                     '''
                 }
             }
         }

    }

8.  Click Save.

IMPORTANT: - This is a simple demonstration pipeline using files copied
into /workspace. It does not perform a Git checkout. - The Checkout
stage verifies that the source files are present. - For a Git-based
CI/CD pipeline, connect a Git repository and replace this stage with a
real checkout step. - The Docker Playground must still be running while
Jenkins executes.

PART F – EXECUTE THE PIPELINE

1.  Open the Python-CI-CD job.
2.  Click Build Now.
3.  Open Build History.
4.  Select the latest build.
5.  Click Console Output.
6.  Confirm that all four stages complete successfully.

Expected stages:

    Checkout
    Build
    Test
    Deploy

Expected final result:

    Finished: SUCCESS

PART G – VERIFY THE DEPLOYED APPLICATION

In the Docker Playground terminal:

    docker ps

Check the application:

    curl http://localhost:5001

Expected output:

    Hello from Jenkins CI/CD Pipeline!

